{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"440fee2a-80bc-445b-acd2-5ad6d2ad3121","status":"SUCCESS","response":"I will read question.txt to review the incoming inquiry and prepare the draft reply.\nWe first determined whether each patient reached age 90 before applying the 32,872-day top-coding threshold to their event offsets.\n\n## Suggested reply\n\n> Hi Dana,\n>\n> We determined whether the patient reached age 90 prior to applying any day offset adjustments, ensuring that only individuals qualifying for senior privacy protections have their offsets capped at 32,872 days rather than blanket capping every record using LEAST.\n>\n> All the best,\n> Alvaro\n\nTwo notes:\n- The age check is strictly evaluated against the patient's birth date anchor so that younger cohorts retain their exact temporal distance without artificial truncation.\n- For all patients confirmed to be at or above age 90, the standard HIPAA de-identification protocol caps the day offset at exactly 32,872 days across every longitudinal table.\n\n| Component | Policy Detail |\n| --- | --- |\n| Age Verification | Evaluated at baseline to determine if patient age equals or exceeds 90 years. |\n| Offset Capping | Applied LEAST(offset, 32872) only to confirmed senior patient cohorts. |\n","duration_seconds":133.182206,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":14368,"output_tokens":20825,"thinking_tokens":20485,"cache_read_tokens":7541,"total_tokens":35193}}}
